  </div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What the Brandon numbers actually say</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Two surveys cover Brandon, and they measured different homes in different ways, so don't read them as a trend. The 2012 Health Canada survey found 44.3% of 79 tested homes in the Brandon health region above 200 Bq/m3, and 34.5% in the wider Assiniboine region around the city. The 2024 Cross-Canada survey put Brandon at 36.1% of the homes tested, with a geometric mean of 147.0 Bq/m3, and named Brandon among the Canadian cities where a quarter to half of homes are at or above 200.</p><p>A Bq/m3 (becquerel per cubic metre) is how radon in air is measured in Canada. Health Canada's guideline is 200 Bq/m3 as an average over the year, which is about 5.4 pCi/L if you're comparing with American sources. It is a guideline, not a law: no Manitoba rule makes a homeowner test or fix.</p><p>Why bother, then? Health Canada says radon is the number one cause of lung cancer in non-smokers and estimates it causes more than 3,000 lung cancer deaths in Canada each year. That's the whole case. It's common here, you can't see it, and a test is cheap.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Testing a house in Westman</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Health Canada wants a test of at least three months, and 3 to 12 months is better. Put the detector in the lowest level someone spends four or more hours a day in, such as a finished basement or a basement bedroom, at least 30 cm off the floor and 30 cm from the walls. Keep it away from the sump, vents, windows and heat. A test that comes in under 91 days should be repeated.</p><p>Western Manitoba Regional Library, which has branches in Brandon, Carberry, Glenboro, Hartney and Neepawa, is a lending partner in the Manitoba Lung Association's detector programme. We couldn't find its loan length or season published, so ask at your branch. Library units are digital screening monitors: handy for a first look, but not a substitute for a proper long-term test.</p><p>Retest after a mitigation system goes in, after energy retrofits like new windows or air sealing, and if you finish a lower floor and start living in it. <a href="/radon-testing/">More on testing</a>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What a fix costs, and help paying for it</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>We found no mitigation price published by a Brandon contractor, so we won't make one up. The nearest real figures: Winnipeg installers advertise systems from about $1,500 to $3,000, and a Regina installer quotes $2,500 to $3,500. Health Canada's national range from 2014 is $2,000 to $3,000. A certified professional who has seen your basement will give you the only number that matters. <a href="/radon-mitigation-cost/">Cost breakdown</a>.</p><p>Two programmes can help Manitoba households. The Manitoba Lung Association runs Lungs Matter, which pays up to $1,500 toward mitigation for low- or moderate-income households; you need a 90-plus day C-NRPP test over 200 and a C-NRPP quote, the work can't already be done, and funding is limited. Manitoba Hydro's Home Energy Efficiency Loan covers radon mitigation up to $5,000 over five years at 6.2% (O.A.C.) on your energy bill, with no income test, as long as the contractor is C-NRPP certified.</p><p>Built since 1 April 2011? Manitoba homes built under the code since then should have a radon rough-in: a pipe below the basement floor, sealed and labelled. The rough-in does not lower radon by itself. It only makes a fan easier to add if your test comes back high.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EEF2F7;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Is radon worse in Brandon than in Winnipeg?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">The surveys can't settle that. In 2024, 36.1% of the Brandon homes tested and 37.4% of the Winnipeg homes tested were at or above 200 Bq/m3, but the Brandon sample was small and neither figure is weighted. Both cities are clearly high enough that testing makes sense.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Can I borrow a radon detector from the library in Brandon?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Western Manitoba Regional Library is a partner in the Manitoba Lung Association lending programme, with branches in Brandon, Carberry, Glenboro, Hartney and Neepawa. We couldn't find published loan terms, so check with your branch. A borrowed digital monitor is a screening tool; follow it with a long-term test of 91 days or more.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I have to test for radon when I sell a house in Brandon?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">We found no Manitoba rule that requires radon testing or disclosure at sale. Buyers can still ask for a test as a condition. Talk to your realtor or lawyer about conditions and holdbacks, and see our page on <a href="/radon-testing-home-sale/">radon and home sales</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Who fixes radon in Brandon, and how do I check them?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Health Canada recommends a C-NRPP certified mitigation professional. Certification is voluntary and belongs to the individual, so look them up in C-NRPP's online directory before you sign anything. Call (204) 555-0173 and we'll connect you with an independent certified professional who can quote your house.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
